# ctrl-exec-agent script allowlist
# /etc/ctrl-exec-agent/scripts.conf
#
# One entry per line:
#   <name> = <absolute-path> [profile=<name>] [input=<mode>] [output=<mode>]
# The name is what the ctrl-exec passes as the script argument.
# The path must be executable and should be owned by root: the script and
# every directory from / down to it owned by root and not writable by group or
# other (install them with e.g. install -o root -g root -m 755). This is
# checked. With script_ownership = warn in agent.conf (the default) an entry
# that breaks the rule still runs; the reason is logged at load and shown by
# `cea self-check` next to the script. With script_ownership = strict it is
# skipped at load ("script not permitted: '<path>' is group-writable", "... is
# owned by uid 1000, not root", "... is world-writable"), listed by
# `cea self-check`, and refused by the executor at run time (exit 126). This
# applies whatever the profile's run_as.
#
# The optional 'profile=<name>' pins the security context the script runs under
# (run_as, capabilities, writable paths). It is order-independent (either side of
# the path); the profile is defined in agent.conf under [profile <name>], and is
# enforced only when the executor is enabled (executor_socket in agent.conf). A
# script with no profile= uses the most-restrictive 'default'. A profile= naming
# a profile not defined in agent.conf is a fatal config error (refuses to serve).
#
# The optional 'input=<mode>' says how confidential run input (the 'input' fields
# of a run request) reaches the script; it is order-independent like profile=:
#   input=context    the stdin context JSON gains an "input" object (default)
#   input=raw        stdin is the bytes of the request's one input field
#   input=raw:<name> stdin is the bytes of the input field <name>
#   input=fields     one 0600 file per field in $CTRL_EXEC_INPUT (a private
#                    directory removed afterwards); stdin is empty
# raw and fields put the context JSON (without the caller's token) in
# $CTRL_EXEC_CONTEXT. Any other value skips the entry. All modes work with and
# without the executor; with it, the executor reads the mode from this file
# itself, and for input=fields it creates the directory as root under
# /run/ctrl-exec-agent/input and gives it to the profile's run_as user.
#
# The optional 'output=<mode>' says whether the run's stdout and stderr may be
# stored; order-independent like profile=:
#   output=stored        stored in the dispatcher's run store (default)
#   output=confidential  returned to the caller in the synchronous response,
#                        marked output_confidential, and never stored: the
#                        dispatcher keeps the exit status only. Async runs of
#                        the script are refused.
# Any other value skips the entry. The dispatcher relies on the mark, so an
# agent older than 0.13 (no mark) has its output stored as before.
#
# A line with more than one path, a relative path, a name outside [A-Za-z0-9_-],
# or a path of 4096 characters or more is skipped. If a name appears twice, the
# last valid line wins; a skipped line never replaces an earlier valid one.
#
# Examples (with and without a profile):
#   backup-mysql = /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts/backup-mysql.sh
#   deploy-app   = /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts/deploy-app.sh
#   deploy-cert  = /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts/deploy-cert.sh   profile=cert-deploy
#   restart-web  = /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts/restart-web.sh   profile=svc-restart
#   install-key  = /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts/install-key.sh   input=fields
#   issue-cert   = /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts/issue-cert.sh    output=confidential

# --- Demonstrator (evaluation and examples) ---
#
# The ctrl-exec-demonstrator script illustrates core ctrl-exec capabilities:
# stdout/stderr capture, exit code propagation, argument passing, JSON context,
# and agent-side information. Run the script directly on the agent host to see
# available subcommands and the ctrl-exec invocations that exercise them.
#
# Uncomment to enable:
# ctrl-exec-demonstrator = /opt/ctrl-exec-scripts/ctrl-exec-demonstrator.sh

# Note: seamless dispatcher cert rotation is built in - the agent handles it as a
# control-plane operation (POST /rotate-serial), not as a script. No allowlist
# entry is needed for rotation.
